---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import InstallLine from '../components/InstallLine.astro';
import { FUNCTIONS, CODE_FUNCTIONS, SURFACES, HOWTOS, FACTS } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { cell, SURFACE_SAMPLES } from '../data/examples.mjs';

const tabs = [
  { label: 'Bash', alias: 'Bash', surface: 'shell' },
  { label: 'Python', alias: 'Python', surface: 'python' },
  { label: 'TypeScript', alias: 'TypeScript', surface: 'typescript' },
  { label: 'Ruby', alias: 'Ruby', surface: 'ruby' },
  { label: 'R', alias: 'R', surface: 'r' },
  { label: 'Rust', alias: 'Rust', surface: 'rust' },
  { label: 'SQL', alias: 'SQL', surface: 'duckdb' },
];

const bash = [cell('filter', 'shell').runs[0]];
const surfaceOf = (slug) => SURFACES.find((s) => s.slug === slug);
const plannedCaption = (slug) => surfaceOf(slug).name + ' comes with 0.1. The calls will read like this.';
const sixHowtos = HOWTOS.slice(0, 6);
---
<Base title="ThinkThen">
  <h1>Code that knows what you mean.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Simple functions that give your software the judgment to handle whatever comes its way. Hand one a message, ask "Is this a complaint?", and get back yes, no, or not sure.</p>

  <p>Your software already branches on an exit code, a pattern, or a column. Now it can branch on a decision about meaning, in one line. No regex that breaks by Friday. No chat model wrapped in a prompt, a parser, a retry loop, and a bill.</p>

  <p><code>decide</code> is an if that understands. <code>filter</code> is a grep that understands. <code>rank</code> is a sort that understands. Seven more are listed below. Every answer comes with a probability, and you set the bar. Anything in the middle comes back as not sure, and your software sends it to a person. In Bash the exit code is 0 for yes, 1 for no, and 3 for not sure.</p>

  <div class="tabbed" data-fallback="Bash">
    <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
      {tabs.map((t) => (
        <button type="button" role="tab" data-lang={t.label} data-alias={t.alias} aria-selected={t.label === 'Bash'}>{t.label}</button>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="cell" data-lang="Bash">
      {bash.map((run) => <Terminal run={run} caption="Recorded from the real command. filter keeps the two complaints." />)}
      <InstallLine line={surfaceOf('shell').install[0][0]} status="planned" note="coming with 0.1" caption="Install the command" />
    </div>
    {tabs.slice(1).map((t) => (
      <div class="cell" data-lang={t.label} hidden>
        <Code code={SURFACE_SAMPLES[t.surface].code} caption={plannedCaption(t.surface)} />
        <InstallLine line={surfaceOf(t.surface).install[0][0]} status="planned" caption="Install, once it ships" />
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/install/">Every way to install it</a> · <a href="/surfaces/">Call it from Python, TypeScript, Ruby, R, Rust, C, or SQL</a></p>

  <h2>The ten functions</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {CODE_FUNCTIONS.map((fn) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/functions/${fn.name}/`}>
        <b>{fn.name}</b>
        <span>{fn.line}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/question-file/">Save a question as a file</a> · <a href="/tutorial/">Start the tutorial</a></p>

  <h2>The model behind the answers</h2>
  <p>Jev makes the decisions. It is a System One model from TypeSafe. It answers in one step. It writes no explanation, and you train nothing. Write a question it has never seen, in plain words, and it returns a probability. About a penny buys a thousand answers. <a href="/backends/">The backends page</a> shows how to name a model and what to do when a new one ships.</p>

  <h2>Jobs your software can take on</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {sixHowtos.map((h) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/how-tos/${h.slug}/`}>
        <b>{h.title}</b>
        <span>{h.reader}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/how-tos/">All eleven how-tos</a> · <a href="/recipes/">Shell recipes</a></p>

  <h2>Test it before you trust it</h2>
  <p>A question is a small file. It lives in your repo and shows up in a pull request. Run it against your own labeled cases before you ship it. Every answer carries the probability the model reported. The band you set sends the middle to a person. Answers are saved. Only what you ask about leaves your machine. The <a href="/trust/">trust page</a> shows how to set the band and check a question against your own cases.</p>
</Base>
